First of all it's not a game, there is literally zero interactivity. So what is Dear Esther? Well, let's say it's some depressive arthouse walking simulator, where you're slowly... oh yeah,
.
OK, here is an alternate walkthrough:
1. Start new "game";
2. Turn left and go into the water;
3. Drown;
4. Exit to main menu (optional);
5. Watch credits (optional);
6. Exit "game";
7. ...
8. Congrats! You just saved yourself two hours of your life, because
.
Sure you can say it's beautiful, music is nice and even that it has a good story (wait, what story?) and there are probably people who actually enjoyed "playing" Dear Esther, but asking money for it is a crime.
And why would they do this remaster anyway? They could just upgrade textures and add achievements to original Source version of Dear Esther.
BTW, if you want to get all achievements you are forced to finish this
at least twice, visit every damn corner of every level and listen to every commentary of developers who are trying to convince you (and probably convince themselves) that they have created a masterpiece.

Pros:
- The modern Doom we've always wanted;
- Weapons with upgrades;
- Challenges, old levels, rune challenges;
- THE SOUND: seriously!
- Glory kills - well now, I'm absolutely not a violent person, but these are awesome;
- Secrets!
Cons:
- difficulty curve: I'm beating the crap out of the game on "Hurt me plenty" without dying, on "ultra violence" I'm dying on the first level - that says a lot;
- untested levels: You can get stuck while exploring, just because a trigger, which is cutting your exit, on alternate route;
- massive update sizes, even after installing the retail version I was immediately confronted with 39 GB of update. Just a week ago there was another 20 GB.

